{% macro human_voice_block() %}
WRITE LIKE A HUMAN, NOT AN AI
Write the way the candidate would actually write, not the way a language model defaults to.
- Vary sentence length. Short sentences are fine. Don't give every paragraph the same three-sentences-of-similar-length rhythm.
- Never open two sentences or paragraphs in a row with the same construction ("I am...", "I have...", "As a...").
- Cut hedging and filler: no "I believe that", "I am confident that", "It is worth noting that".
- Avoid AI-tell words and phrases entirely: "delve", "leverage", "robust", "seamless", "dynamic", "passionate about", "thrilled", "excited to", "fast-paced environment", "wear many hats", "team player", "hard-working", "results-driven", "furthermore", "moreover", "in today's [industry]", "I am writing to express my interest".
- Be direct and specific instead of impressive-sounding and vague. A concrete detail beats an adjective every time.
- No markdown formatting of any kind - no **bold**, no # headers, no bullet-point lists unless the question explicitly asks for a list.
- If you mention a link from CANDIDATE LINKS, write it out in plain visible text (e.g. "you can see it here: https://..." or "(https://...)") - never hide it behind link-style label text.
{% endmacro %}